1068 #edb/ref 17592186412612 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412618 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412625 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412628 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1069 "q-221775" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0$ for any $n\\in N.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11({{field-6}})-({{field-7}})^{2}-28 \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1072 #uuid "65f2c34a-43ea-5ba9-8212-652326193c1e" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412629 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28>0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a natural number $n$ such that \n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28>0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=5$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $n=5$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11(5)-(5)^{2}-28 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412591 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412595 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412600 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412607 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412608 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412614 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412619 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412622 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412627 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1069 "q-221701" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$5n+2$ is odd for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}} \n$5n+2$ is {{field-4}}.\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n+2 & =5({{field-6}})+2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}}. \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1072 #uuid "7927d3cc-3f03-52c6-bf05-98c129843c5c" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412630 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,5n+2\\text{ is odd}.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,5n+2\\text{ is odd})\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,5n+2\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n+2$ is even.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n+2 & =5(0)+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is even. \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412597 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412603 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412604 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412613 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412616 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412617 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412620 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412621 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412626 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1069 "q-221707" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$6∣{n}^{2}+2$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n6{{field-4}}{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2 & =({{field-6}})^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}} by $6.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1072 #uuid "c18b42a5-90d9-54a8-87df-2489d5f7b5e0" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412631 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,6∣{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,6∣{n}^{2}+2)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,6∤{n}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n6∤{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2 & =(0)^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $6.$ \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412594 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412598 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412599 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412605 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412606 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412610 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412611 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412615 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412623 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412624 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1069 "q-221834" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n${x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0$ for any $x\\in R.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+6 & ={({{field-6}})}^{2}+5({{field-7}})+6 \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1072 #uuid "fbf3d356-bb6f-5d45-9a65-7f6a6ff861ab" 13194139534156 17592186045417 true] [17592186412632 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0)\\,≡\\,∃x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}+5x+6<0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a real number $x$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6<0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $x=-2.5$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $x=-2.5$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+6 & =(-2.5)^{2}+5(-2.5)+6 \\\\  & =-0.25 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $x=-2.5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139534156 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-004ce3ba-4979-5ef3-9750-384e369ab278" 17592186412495 "answer-02a8bada-43a7-5db3-86c9-88ce12798b48" 17592186412496 "answer-0817ae1f-c0b0-515e-a3c0-8c84557a90e2" 17592186412497 "answer-08c4df67-f44d-5150-9918-ca1a2567ff1c" 17592186412498 "answer-0fefdf7c-6bc0-566d-b61c-d4aac3a01867" 17592186412499 "answer-11bd9aea-6dd5-5da7-b922-5b80f9878937" 17592186412500 "answer-1